"SID Strings - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthz/sid-strings)\nciteturn20view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthz/sid-strings\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 148\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience L1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: cite2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com cite3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge L7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents cite1†Read in English Add Add to Plans cite4†Edit†github.com L13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try cite1†signing in or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # SID Strings\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: ## In this article\nL30: \nL31: In the cite5†security descriptor definition language (SDDL), cite6†security descriptor string use SID strings for the following components of a cite7†security descriptor :\nL32: \nL33:   * Owner\nL34:   * Primary group\nL35:   * The cite8†trustee in an ACE\nL36: \nL37: A SID string in a security descriptor string can use either the standard string representation of a SID (S-R-I-S-S ) or one of the string constants defined in Sddl.h. For more information about the standard SID string notation, see cite9†SID Components .\nL38: The following SID string constants for well-known SIDs are defined in Sddl.h. For information about the corresponding cite10†relative IDs (RIDs), see cite11†Well-known SIDs .\nL39: SDDL SID string  | Constant in Sddl.h  | Account alias and corresponding RID\nL40: --- | --- | ---\nL41: \"AA\"  | SDDL_ACCESS_CONTROL_ASSISTANCE_OPS  | Access control assistance operators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_ACCESS_CONTROL_ASSISTANCE_OPS. Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL42: \"AC\"  | SDDL_ALL_APP_PACKAGES  | All applications running in an app package context. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_BUILTIN_PACKAGE_ANY_PACKAGE. Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL43: \"AN\"  | SDDL_ANONYMOUS  | Anonymous logon. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_ANONYMOUS_LOGON_RID.\nL44: \"AO\"  | SDDL_ACCOUNT_OPERATORS  | Account operators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_ACCOUNT_OPS.\nL45: \"AP\"  | SDDL_PROTECTED_USERS  | cite12†Protected Users . The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_PROTECTED_USERS. Windows Server 2012, Windows 8, Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL46: \"AU\"  | SDDL_AUTHENTICATED_USERS  | Authenticated users. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_AUTHENTICATED_USER_RID.\nL47: \"BA\"  | SDDL_BUILTIN_ADMINISTRATORS  | Built-in administrators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_ADMINS.\nL48: \"BG\"  | SDDL_BUILTIN_GUESTS  | Built-in guests. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_GUESTS.\nL49: \"BO\"  | SDDL_BACKUP_OPERATORS  | Backup operators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_BACKUP_OPS.\nL50: \"BU\"  | SDDL_BUILTIN_USERS  | Built-in users. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_USERS.\nL51: \"CA\"  | SDDL_CERT_SERV_ADMINISTRATORS  | Certificate publishers. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_CERT_ADMINS.\nL52: \"CD\"  | SDDL_CERTSVC_DCOM_ACCESS  | Users who can connect to certification authorities using Distributed Component Object Model (DCOM). The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_CERTSVC_DCOM_ACCESS_GROUP. Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL53: \"CG\"  | SDDL_CREATOR_GROUP  | Creator group. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_CREATOR_GROUP_RID.\nL54: \"CN\"  | SDDL_CLONEABLE_CONTROLLERS  | Cloneable domain controllers. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_CLONEABLE_CONTROLLERS. Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL55: \"CO\"  | SDDL_CREATOR_OWNER  | Creator owner. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_CREATOR_OWNER_RID.\nL56: \"CY\"  | SDDL_CRYPTO_OPERATORS  | Crypto operators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_CRYPTO_OPERATORS. Windows Server 2003:* Not available.\nL57: \"DA\"  | SDDL_DOMAIN_ADMINISTRATORS  | Domain administrators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_ADMINS.\nL58: \"DC\"  | SDDL_DOMAIN_COMPUTERS  | Domain computers. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_COMPUTERS.\nL59: \"DD\"  | SDDL_DOMAIN_DOMAIN_CONTROLLERS  | Domain controllers. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_CONTROLLERS.\nL60: \"DG\"  | SDDL_DOMAIN_GUESTS  | Domain guests. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_GUESTS.\nL61: \"DU\"  | SDDL_DOMAIN_USERS  | Domain users. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_USERS.\nL62: \"EA\"  | SDDL_ENTERPRISE_ADMINS  | Enterprise administrators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_ENTERPRISE_ADMINS.\nL63: \"ED\"  | SDDL_ENTERPRISE_DOMAIN_CONTROLLERS  | Enterprise domain controllers. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_SERVER_LOGON_RID.\nL64: \"EK\"  | SDDL_ENTERPRISE_KEY_ADMINS  | Enterprise key admins. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_ENTERPRISE_KEY_ADMINS. Windows Server 2012 R2, Windows 8.1, Windows Server 2012, Windows 8, Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL65: \"ER\"  | SDDL_EVENT_LOG_READERS  | Event log readers. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_EVENT_LOG_READERS_GROUP. Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL66: \"ES\"  | SDDL_RDS_ENDPOINT_SERVERS  | Endpoint servers. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_RDS_ENDPOINT_SERVERS. Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL67: \"HA\"  | SDDL_HYPER_V_ADMINS  | Hyper-V administrators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_HYPER_V_ADMINS. Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL68: \"HI\"  | SDDL_ML_HIGH  | High integrity level. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_MANDATORY_HIGH_RID. Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL69: \"HO\"  | SDDL_USER_MODE_HARDWARE_OPERATORS  | Group members may operate hardware from user mode. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_USER_MODE_HARDWARE_OPERATORS.\nL70: \"IS\"  | SDDL_IIS_USERS  | Anonymous Internet users. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_IUSERS. Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL71: \"IU\"  | SDDL_INTERACTIVE  | Interactively logged-on user. This is a group identifier added to the token of a process when it was logged on interactively. The corresponding logon type is LOGON32_LOGON_INTERACTIVE. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_INTERACTIVE_RID.\nL72: \"KA\"  | SDDL_KEY_ADMINS  | Domain key admins. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_KEY_ADMINS. Windows Server 2012 R2, Windows 8.1, Windows Server 2012, Windows 8, Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL73: \"LA\"  | SDDL_LOCAL_ADMIN  | Local administrator. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_USER_RID_ADMIN.\nL74: \"LG\"  | SDDL_LOCAL_GUEST  | Local guest. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_USER_RID_GUEST.\nL75: \"LS\"  | SDDL_LOCAL_SERVICE  | Local service account. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_LOCAL_SERVICE_RID.\nL76: \"LU\"  | SDDL_PERFLOG_USERS  | Performance Log users. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_LOGGING_USERS.\nL77: \"LW\"  | SDDL_ML_LOW  | Low integrity level. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_MANDATORY_LOW_RID. Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL78: \"ME\"  | SDDL_ML_MEDIUM  | Medium integrity level. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_MANDATORY_MEDIUM_RID. Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL79: \"MP\"  | SDDL_ML_MEDIUM_PLUS  | Medium Plus integrity level. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_MANDATORY_MEDIUM_PLUS_RID. Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL80: \"MU\"  | SDDL_PERFMON_USERS  | Performance Monitor users. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_MONITORING_USERS.\nL81: \"NO\"  | SDDL_NETWORK_CONFIGURATION_OPS  | Network configuration operators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_NETWORK_CONFIGURATION_OPS.\nL82: \"NS\"  | SDDL_NETWORK_SERVICE  | Network service account. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_NETWORK_SERVICE_RID.\nL83: \"NU\"  | SDDL_NETWORK  | Network logon user. This is a group identifier added to the token of a process when it was logged on across a network. The corresponding logon type is LOGON32_LOGON_NETWORK. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_NETWORK_RID.\nL84: \"OW\"  | SDDL_OWNER_RIGHTS  | Owner Rights SID. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_CREATOR_OWNER_RIGHTS_RID. Windows Server 2003:* Not available.\nL85: \"PA\"  | SDDL_GROUP_POLICY_ADMINS  | Group Policy administrators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_POLICY_ADMINS.\nL86: \"PO\"  | SDDL_PRINTER_OPERATORS  | Printer operators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_PRINT_OPS\nL87: \"PS\"  | SDDL_PERSONAL_SELF  | Principal self. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_PRINCIPAL_SELF_RID.\nL88: \"PU\"  | SDDL_POWER_USERS  | Power users. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_POWER_USERS.\nL89: \"RA\"  | SDDL_RDS_REMOTE_ACCESS_SERVERS  | RDS remote access servers. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_RDS_REMOTE_ACCESS_SERVERS. Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL90: \"RC\"  | SDDL_RESTRICTED_CODE  | Restricted code. This is a restricted token created using the cite13†CreateRestrictedToken function. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_RESTRICTED_CODE_RID.\nL91: \"RD\"  | SDDL_REMOTE_DESKTOP  | Terminal server users. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_REMOTE_DESKTOP_USERS.\nL92: \"RE\"  | SDDL_REPLICATOR  | Replicator. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_REPLICATOR.\nL93: \"RM\"  | SDDL_RMS__SERVICE_OPERATORS  | RMS Service. Available only in Windows Vista.\nL94: \"RO\"  | SDDL_ENTERPRISE_RO_DCs  | Enterprise Read-only domain controllers. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_ENTERPRISE_READONLY_DOMAIN_CONTROLLERS. Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL95: \"RS\"  | SDDL_RAS_SERVERS  | RAS servers group. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_RAS_SERVERS.\nL96: \"RU\"  | SDDL_ALIAS_PREW2KCOMPACC  | Alias to grant permissions to accounts that use applications compatible with operating systems previous to Windows 2000. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_PREW2KCOMPACCESS.\nL97: \"SA\"  | SDDL_SCHEMA_ADMINISTRATORS  | Schema administrators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_SCHEMA_ADMINS.\nL98: \"SH\"  | SDDL_OPENSSH_USERS  | Users allowed to connect with OpenSSH. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_OPENSSH_USERS.\nL99: \"SI\"  | SDDL_ML_SYSTEM  | System integrity level. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_MANDATORY_SYSTEM_RID. Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL100: \"SO\"  | SDDL_SERVER_OPERATORS  | Server operators. The corresponding RID is DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_SYSTEM_OPS.\nL101: \"SS\"  | SDDL_SERVICE_ASSERTED  | Authentication service asserted. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_AUTHENTICATION_SERVICE_ASSERTED_RID. Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL102: \"SU\"  | SDDL_SERVICE  | Service logon user. This is a group identifier added to the token of a process when it was logged as a service. The corresponding logon type is LOGON32_LOGON_SERVICE. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_SERVICE_RID.\nL103: \"SY\"  | SDDL_LOCAL_SYSTEM  | Local system. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_LOCAL_SYSTEM_RID.\nL104: \"UD\"  | SDDL_USER_MODE_DRIVERS  | User-mode driver. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_USERMODEDRIVERHOST_ID_BASE_RID. Windows Server 2008 R2, Windows 7, Windows Server 2008, Windows Vista and Windows Server 2003: Not available.\nL105: \"WD\"  | SDDL_EVERYONE  | Everyone. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_WORLD_RID.\nL106: \"WR\"  | SDDL_WRITE_RESTRICTED_CODE  | Write Restricted code. The corresponding RID is SECURITY_WRITE_RESTRICTED_CODE_RID. Windows Server 2003:* Not available.\nL107: The cite14†ConvertSidToStringSid and cite15†ConvertStringSidToSid functions always use the standard SID string notation and do not support SDDL SID string constants.\nL108: \nL109: For more information about well-known SIDs, see cite11†Well-known SIDs .\nL110: ## See also\nL111: \nL112: cite16†[MS-DTYP]: Security Descriptor Description Language L113: \nL114: * * *\nL115: \nL116: ## Feedback\nL117: \nL118: Was this page helpful?\nL119: \nL120: Yes No No\nL121: \nL122: Need help with this topic?\nL123: \nL124: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL125: \nL126: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL127: \nL128: Suggest a fix?\nL129: \nL130: * * *\nL131: \nL132: ## Additional resources\nL133: \nL134: * * *\nL135: \nL136:   * Last updated on 2023-09-26\nL137: ### In this article\nL138: \nL139: Was this page helpful?\nL140: \nL141: Need help with this topic?\nL142: \nL143: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL144: \nL145: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL146: \nL147: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFile Access Rights Constants (WinNT.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/file-access-rights-constants)\nciteturn20view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/file-access-rights-constants\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 142\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience L1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: cite2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com cite3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge L7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents cite1†Read in English Add Add to Plans cite4†Edit†github.com L13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try cite1†signing in or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # File Access Rights Constants\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: ## In this article\nL30: \nL31: The valid access rights for files and directories include the DELETE, READ_CONTROL, WRITE_DAC, WRITE_OWNER, and SYNCHRONIZE cite5†standard access rights . The following table lists the access rights that are specific to files and directories.\nL32: \nL33: Constant/value  | Description\nL34: --- | ---\nL35: \nL36: FILE_ADD_FILE\nL37: 2\nL38:  | For a directory, the right to create a file in the directory.\nL39: \nL40: FILE_ADD_SUBDIRECTORY\nL41: 4\nL42:  | For a directory, the right to create a subdirectory.\nL43: FILE_ALL_ACCESS\nL44:  | All possible access rights for a file.\nL45: \nL46: FILE_APPEND_DATA\nL47: 4\nL48:  | For a file object, the right to append data to the file. (For local files, write operations will not overwrite existing data if this flag is specified without FILE_WRITE_DATA.) For a directory object, the right to create a subdirectory (FILE_ADD_SUBDIRECTORY).\nL49: \nL50: FILE_CREATE_PIPE_INSTANCE\nL51: 4\nL52:  | For a named pipe, the right to create a pipe.\nL53: FILE_DELETE_CHILD\nL54: 64 (0x40)\nL55:  | For a directory, the right to delete a directory and all the files it contains, including read-only files.\nL56: \nL57: FILE_EXECUTE\nL58: 32 (0x20)\nL59:  | For a native code file, the right to execute the file. This access right given to scripts may cause the script to be executable, depending on the script interpreter.\nL60: \nL61: FILE_LIST_DIRECTORY\nL62: 1\nL63:  | For a directory, the right to list the contents of the directory.\nL64: \nL65: FILE_READ_ATTRIBUTES\nL66: 128 (0x80)\nL67:  | The right to read file attributes.\nL68: FILE_READ_DATA\nL69: 1\nL70:  | For a file object, the right to read the corresponding file data. For a directory object, the right to read the corresponding directory data.\nL71: \nL72: FILE_READ_EA\nL73: 8\nL74:  | The right to read extended file attributes.\nL75: \nL76: FILE_TRAVERSE\nL77: 32 (0x20)\nL78:  | For a directory, the right to traverse the directory. By default, users are assigned the BYPASS_TRAVERSE_CHECKING cite6†privilege , which ignores the FILE_TRAVERSE cite7†access right . See the remarks in cite8†File Security and Access Rights for more information.\nL79: FILE_WRITE_ATTRIBUTES\nL80: 256 (0x100)\nL81:  | The right to write file attributes.\nL82: \nL83: FILE_WRITE_DATA\nL84: 2\nL85:  | For a file object, the right to write data to the file. For a directory object, the right to create a file in the directory (FILE_ADD_FILE).\nL86: \nL87: FILE_WRITE_EA\nL88: 16 (0x10)\nL89:  | The right to write extended file attributes.\nL90: \nL91: STANDARD_RIGHTS_READ\nL92:  | Includes READ_CONTROL, which is the right to read the information in the file or directory object's security descriptor. This does not include the information in the SACL.\nL93: STANDARD_RIGHTS_WRITE\nL94:  | Same as STANDARD_RIGHTS_READ.\nL95: ## Requirements\nL96: \nL97: Requirement  | Value\nL98: --- | ---\nL99: Minimum supported client\nL100:  | Windows XP [desktop apps only]\nL101: Minimum supported server\nL102:  | Windows Server 2003 [desktop apps only]\nL103: Header\nL104:  |\nL105: \nL106: WinNT.h (include Windows.h)\nL107: \nL108: * * *\nL109: \nL110: ## Feedback\nL111: \nL112: Was this page helpful?\nL113: \nL114: Yes No No\nL115: \nL116: Need help with this topic?\nL117: \nL118: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL119: \nL120: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL121: \nL122: Suggest a fix?\nL123: \nL124: * * *\nL125: \nL126: ## Additional resources\nL127: \nL128: * * *\nL129: \nL130:   * Last updated on 2021-01-07\nL131: ### In this article\nL132: \nL133: Was this page helpful?\nL134: \nL135: Need help with this topic?\nL136: \nL137: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL138: \nL139: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL140: \nL141: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGetApplicationRestartSettings function (winbase.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-getapplicationrestartsettings)\nciteturn20view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-getapplicationrestartsettings\",\"lineno\":90}); Total lines: 207\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience L1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: cite2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com cite3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge L7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents cite1†Read in English Add Add to Plans cite4†Edit†github.com L13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try cite1†signing in or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # GetApplicationRestartSettings function (winbase.h)\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: Retrieves the restart information registered for the specified process.\nL33: \nL34: ## Syntax\nL35: \nL36:     `HRESULT GetApplicationRestartSettings(\nL37:       [in]            HANDLE hProcess,\nL38:       [out, optional] PWSTR  pwzCommandline,\nL39:       [in, out]       PDWORD pcchSize,\nL40:       [out, optional] PDWORD pdwFlags\nL41:     );\nL42:     `\nL43: ## Parameters\nL44: \nL45: `[in] hProcess`\nL46: \nL47: A handle to the process. This handle must have the PROCESS_VM_READ access right.\nL48: \nL49: `[out, optional] pwzCommandline`\nL50: \nL51: A pointer to a buffer that receives the restart command line specified by the application when it called the cite5†RegisterApplicationRestart function. The maximum size of the command line, in characters, is RESTART_MAX_CMD_LINE. Can be NULL if pcchSize is zero.\nL52: \nL53: `[in, out] pcchSize`\nL54: \nL55: On input, specifies the size of the pwzCommandLine buffer, in characters.\nL56: If the buffer is not large enough to receive the command line, the function fails with HRESULT_FROM_WIN32(ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER) and sets this parameter to the required buffer size, in characters.\nL57: \nL58: On output, specifies the size of the buffer that was used.\nL59: To determine the required buffer size, set pwzCommandLine to NULL and this parameter to zero. The size includes one for the null-terminator character. Note that the function returns S_OK, not HRESULT_FROM_WIN32(ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER) in this case.\nL60: \nL61: `[out, optional] pdwFlags`\nL62: \nL63: A pointer to a variable that receives the flags specified by the application when it called the cite5†RegisterApplicationRestart function.\nL64: ## Return value\nL65: \nL66: This function returns S_OK on success or one of the following error codes.\nL67: \nL68: Return code  | Description\nL69: --- | ---\nL70: \nL71: E_INVALIDARG\nL72:  | One or more parameters are not valid.\nL73: \nL74: HRESULT_FROM_WIN32(ERROR_NOT_FOUND)\nL75:  | The application did not register for restart.\nL76: \nL77: HRESULT_FROM_WIN32(ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER)\nL78:  | The pwzCommandLine buffer is too small. The function returns the required buffer size in pcchSize. Use the required size to reallocate the buffer.\nL79: ## Remarks\nL80: \nL81: This information is available only for the current process; you cannot call this function after your program is restarted to get the restart command line. To get the command line after a restart, access the argv parameter of your main function.\nL82: #### Examples\nL83: The following example shows how to get the restart settings specified when you called the cite5†RegisterApplicationRestart function.\nL84: \nL85:     `#include <windows.h>\nL86:     #include <stdio.h>\nL87: \nL88: \nL89:     void wmain(int argc, WCHAR* argv[])\nL90:     {\nL91:         HRESULT hr = S_OK;\nL92:         WCHAR wsCommandLine[RESTART_MAX_CMD_LINE + 1];\nL93:         DWORD cchCmdLine = sizeof(wsCommandLine) / sizeof(WCHAR);\nL94:         DWORD dwFlags = 0;\nL95:         LPWSTR pwsCmdLine = NULL;\nL96:         UNREFERENCED_PARAMETER(argv);\nL97:         UNREFERENCED_PARAMETER(argc);\nL98: \nL99:         wprintf(L\"Registering for restart...\\n\");\nL100:         hr = RegisterApplicationRestart(L\"/restart -f .\\\\filename.ext\", 0);\nL101:         if (FAILED(hr))\nL102:         {\nL103:             wprintf(L\"RegisterApplicationRestart failed, 0x%x\\n\", hr);\nL104:             goto cleanup;\nL105:         }\nL106: \nL107:         wprintf(L\"Get restart command line using static buffer...\\n\");\nL108:         hr = GetApplicationRestartSettings(GetCurrentProcess(), wsCommandLine, &cchCmdLine, &dwFlags);\nL109:         if (FAILED(hr))\nL110:         {\nL111:             wprintf(L\"GetApplicationRestartSettings failed, 0x%x\\n\", hr);\nL112:             goto cleanup;\nL113:         }\nL114: \nL115:         wprintf(L\"Command line: %s\\n\", wsCommandLine);\nL116: \nL117:         wprintf(L\"\\nGet settings using dynamic buffer...\\n\");\nL118: \nL119:         cchCmdLine = 0;\nL120: \nL121:         // Returns S_OK instead of ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER when pBuffer is NULL and size is 0.\nL122:         hr = GetApplicationRestartSettings(GetCurrentProcess(), (PWSTR)pwsCmdLine, &cchCmdLine, &dwFlags);\nL123:         if (SUCCEEDED(hr))\nL124:         {\nL125:             pwsCmdLine = (LPWSTR)malloc(cchCmdLine * sizeof(WCHAR));\nL126: \nL127:             if (pwsCmdLine)\nL128:             {\nL129:                 hr = GetApplicationRestartSettings(GetCurrentProcess(), (PWSTR)pwsCmdLine, &cchCmdLine, &dwFlags);\nL130:                 if (FAILED(hr))\nL131:                 {\nL132:                     wprintf(L\"GetApplicationRestartSettings failed with 0x%x\\n\", hr);\nL133:                     goto cleanup;\nL134:                 }\nL135:                 wprintf(L\"Command line: %s\\n\", pwsCmdLine);\nL136:             }\nL137:             else\nL138:             {\nL139:                 wprintf(L\"Allocating the command-line buffer failed.\\n\");\nL140:             }\nL141:         }\nL142:         else\nL143:         {\nL144:             if (hr != HRESULT_FROM_WIN32(ERROR_NOT_FOUND)) // Not a restart.\nL145:             {\nL146:                 wprintf(L\"GetApplicationRestartSettings failed with 0x%x\\n\", hr);\nL147:                 goto cleanup;\nL148:             }\nL149:         }\nL150: \nL151:     cleanup:\nL152: \nL153:         if (pwsCmdLine)\nL154:             free(pwsCmdLine);\nL155:     }\nL156: \nL157:     `\nL158: ## Requirements\nL159: \nL160: Requirement  | Value\nL161: --- | ---\nL162: Minimum supported client  | Windows Vista [desktop apps only]\nL163: Minimum supported server  | Windows Server 2008 [desktop apps only]\nL164: Target Platform  | Windows\nL165: Header  | winbase.h (include Windows.h)\nL166: Library  | Kernel32.lib\nL167: DLL  | Kernel32.dll\nL168: \nL169: ## See also\nL170: \nL171: cite5†RegisterApplicationRestart L172: \nL173: * * *\nL174: ## Feedback\nL175: \nL176: Was this page helpful?\nL177: \nL178: Yes No No\nL179: \nL180: Need help with this topic?\nL181: \nL182: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL183: \nL184: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL185: \nL186: Suggest a fix?\nL187: \nL188: * * *\nL189: \nL190: ## Additional resources\nL191: \nL192: * * *\nL193: \nL194:   * Last updated on 2024-02-22\nL195: \nL196: ### In this article\nL197: \nL198: Was this page helpful?\nL199: \nL200: Need help with this topic?\nL201: \nL202: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL203: \nL204: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL205: \nL206: Suggest a fix?\n"
